+# 0001 — CSV import: merge semantics and file constraints
+
+Date: 2026-10-29 · Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Sales imports lead spreadsheets with `import_contacts`. Rows often describe contacts that
+already exist, and spreadsheets are frequently sparse (no phone, partial tags). Persistence
+adapters and any later bulk-load paths must behave the same way, so the rules are fixed here.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Email (normalised) is the identity. A row whose email exists merges into that contact.
+- Merge: the file's name replaces ours; phone is replaced only by a non-empty, valid value
+  (an empty cell, a missing column or an invalid phone never wipes it); tags are added, never
+  removed. An import never deletes contacts.
+- Rows are all-or-nothing: a row with any error is not applied, not even partly. Valid rows
+  in the same file are still applied. An invalid phone is a warning, not an error.
+- Within one file, the first row with an email claims it; later rows with that email are
+  errors.
+- Whole-file problems (`ImportFileError`) write nothing: non-UTF-8 bytes (no encoding
+  fallback), empty file, missing required columns, malformed quoting, more than 1000 contact
+  rows.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- An import cannot be used to clear a phone or remove tags; that stays a per-contact edit.
+- Files saved in legacy Excel "CSV" (cp1252) are rejected; users must re-save as "CSV UTF-8".
+- Large lists must be split into files of at most 1000 contact rows.
